{% doc %}
  Records that a file arrived on a message.

  The file itself is not stored yet, and the schema's upload property stays
  empty. platformOS writes an upload either from a browser that presigned
  an S3 URL, or from content the server generated. A file posted to a page
  is neither: it reaches Liquid as a Rails UploadedFile, which has no
  Liquid representation at all, so it cannot be read, compared or passed on.

  Recording the name and type is still worth doing. An agent reading the
  timeline can see that the customer attached hprb-error.png and ask for it
  again, which beats the email looking like it had nothing on it. A blank
  file is how the timeline knows to say so.

  @param {string} message_id - The message the file arrived on
  @param {string} [filename] - The name from attachment-info
  @param {string} [content_type] - The type from attachment-info
  @param {number} [size] - Bytes, when the provider reports them
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  if message_id == blank
    log 'ERROR: missing message_id in modules/support/commands/attachments/create'
    return nil
  endif

  graphql created = 'modules/support/attachments/create', message_id: message_id, filename: filename, size: size, content_type: content_type
  return created.record
%}
